What is the difference between nuclear fission and nuclear fusion? Give one example of each.

Nuclear fission and nuclear fusion are two fundamental processes in nuclear physics that occur in atomic nuclei.

Nuclear fission, also known as nuclear synthesis, is a process in which atomic nuclei combine to form heavier nuclei. This reaction releases a large amount of energy in the form of heat, light, and radiation. The most common type of nuclear fission reaction is the beta particle emission, where an electron is emitted from the nucleus, resulting in a high-energy electron (beta particle) that travels through space.

Nuclear fusion, on the other hand, is a process in which atomic nuclei combine to form a heavier nucleus. This reaction releases a large amount of energy in the form of light and heat, as well as a vast amount of energy in the form of a new nucleus. The most common type of nuclear fusion reaction is the proton-proton (P-P) reaction, where two atomic nuclei combine to form a single heavier nucleus.

